  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
